WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.940
Lot to talk about.

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:03.370
Of course we're still in the
midst of a global pandemic.

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:05.250
Which is why we're here in this theater.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.510
And you may have seen a
lot of the critical data

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:10.300
being charted and graphed by the CDC.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.440
Now I mentioned this recently,

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.010
some of these graphs,

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:15.440
it's so confusing.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.070
We're getting bombarded
with graphs all the time.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:21.470
You don't know what they
mean and adding to that,

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.970
some of the graphs people are putting out

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.900
just seem a little unnecessary.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.220
For example, check this one out.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.130
There's been a 60% increase

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.263
in people eating morning lasagna.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.700
Is that something we need to know?

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.273
I know it's a good idea.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:38.560
True.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.460
Check this out, a 550%
increase in women named Karen

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:44.193
going by their middle name.

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:46.010
Yeah.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.080
There's a seven thousand percent increase

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.303
in Americans googling
Canadian citizenship.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:54.170
There's an 80% increase

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:57.424
in rooting for the
sharks during shark week.

00:00:57.424 --> 00:00:59.460
(laughter)

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:00.730
We're all sick of humans right now.

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:01.832
Yeah, come on.

00:01:01.832 --> 00:01:04.063
Let the sharks have one.

00:01:04.063 --> 00:01:05.710
(laughs)
Just one shark week,

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:06.830
let the sharks win.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:08.380
You're right.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:09.990
Let's hope they win this week.

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:14.883
Hey, 75% increase in board
game related stabbings.

00:01:14.883 --> 00:01:16.600
I can understand that.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:17.640
[Andy] Yeah.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.477
A 600% increase in
adults overheard yelling,

00:01:20.477 --> 00:01:23.387
"Hope your haircut was worth it, dad!"

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:26.270
That was kind of grime one.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:27.103
Yeah, yeah.

00:01:27.103 --> 00:01:29.020
Unless dad cut his own hair.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.770
Hey, this one just says data.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:32.890
Don't know what to do with that one.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:33.723
Hey, check this out.

00:01:33.723 --> 00:01:35.570
A steady increase in COVID infections

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:38.543
per item of Tommy Bahama clothing owned.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:40.822
That one will get cut.

00:01:40.822 --> 00:01:43.230
(laughter)

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:44.387
Here we see the answer to the question,

00:01:44.387 --> 00:01:47.100
"Why are we having cereal
for dinner, mommy?"

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.610
20% because mommy's on a Zoom,

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:52.653
80% because mommy had gin for breakfast.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.040
Mommy's an alcoholic,
that's the joke there.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:58.480
Is that one to end on?

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.904
Maybe not, but we're
in an abandoned theater

00:02:00.904 --> 00:02:02.504
and we're losing our minds.

00:02:02.504 --> 00:02:05.580
(laughs) Don't say abandoned.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:06.840
The owner's right here.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.180
It's not abandoned.
It's abandoned.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:10.784
Jesus, he doesn't own
anything but that Cadillac

00:02:10.784 --> 00:02:12.233
and it's probably rented.

00:02:13.237 --> 00:02:16.113
"Have a Genesis at nine in
the morning," says Flanny.

